  const revisions = {"after-always":{"after-always-49":{"chapter_number":49,"title":"A Night Without Explanations","content":"The dinner was soup, slightly burnt bread, and a very poor argument about punctuation.\n\nSeverus was winning.\n\nHe had found a note I'd written for the Moonroot records cabinet. I had put a comma in a place he considered offensive. He had circled it twice and written \"WHY?\" in the margin.\n\n\"I thought we were having a quiet evening,\" I said.\n\n\"We are.\"\n\n\"Then why is my comma on trial?\"\n\n\"Because it committed a crime.\"\n\nI stared at him.\n\nHe looked perfectly serious.\n\nIt was one of the reasons I loved him.\n\nWe'd planned the evening two weeks earlier. No archive work after six. No visitors unless the house was on fire. No promises that every difficult feeling had to be discussed before we could have dinner. Severus had chosen the soup. I had bought the bread. Both of us had underestimated how quickly bread could burn when we became distracted by an argument about semicolons.\n\nHe cut away the darkest edge.\n\n\"There,\" he said. \"Salvaged.\"\n\n\"You sound like Tamsin.\"\n\n\"An alarming comparison.\"\n\nI poured the soup.\n\nIt wasn't elegant. The table was too small for the good dishes, the room was chilly, and the rain had begun tapping against the windows. The ordinary life of Spinner's End pressed close around us.\n\nFor once, we let it.\n\nAfter dinner, Severus took a book from the shelf and sat in the chair by the lamp. I settled on the sofa with my green notebook. The pages still held the list I'd written after finding my old room: things I wanted, things I was afraid to ask for, small choices that belonged to me.\n\nI wrote one sentence.\n\nI want tonight to remain ordinary.\n\nSeverus looked up.\n\n\"What are you writing?\"\n\n\"A sentence.\"\n\n\"That explains very little.\"\n\n\"I know.\"\n\nHe raised an eyebrow.\n\nI smiled and closed the notebook.\n\nHe returned to his book.\n\nThe quiet was not empty. It had the sounds of a house occupied by people who didn't have to entertain each other: paper turning, the occasional creak of the old floor, rainwater in the gutter, the kettle beginning to cool.\n\nThe clock reached the half hour, and Severus turned a page without moving his eyes.\n\nI had seen him read too often not to notice.\n\n\"You're thinking about the archive.\"\n\n\"No.\"\n\n\"Your book has been upside down for the last three minutes.\"\n\nHe looked at the cover.\n\nIt was, in fact, upside down.\n\n\"I am conducting an experiment,\" he said.\n\n\"On whether I can be convinced you aren't working?\"\n\n\"On whether you notice unnecessary details.\"\n\n\"Apparently I do.\"\n\nHe straightened the book but didn't open it.\n\n\"Orin sent a corrected index this afternoon. There is a gap between the flood invoices and the keeper's testimony.\"\n\n\"Can the gap wait?\"\n\n\"It has waited more than a century.\"\n\nI set the notebook beside me. \"Then the next hour might not do it further harm.\"\n\nHe looked toward the rain.\n\n\"I told you I wanted an ordinary evening. I appear to be very bad at having one.\"\n\n\"You're allowed to be bad at something.\"\n\n\"An unfamiliar mercy.\"\n\nI laughed, but he didn't.\n\nThere was still something he was trying to ask without quite making the request. His fingers rested on the book's cover, smoothing a crease.\n\n\"Do you want me to listen to what's bothering you,\" I asked, \"or would you prefer that we keep tonight free of work?\"\n\nHe considered.\n\n\"I don't know.\"\n\n\"Then perhaps we could decide in ten minutes. No deadline for an answer.\"\n\nHe gave me a doubtful look.\n\n\"You are making a schedule for not scheduling.\"\n\n\"An impressive achievement.\"\n\nA tiny smile crossed his mouth.\n\nThe kettle clicked as it cooled. I looked around the narrow room: the threadbare rug, the chipped cup I'd begun using without asking whether it was one of his good ones, the shelf I'd never rearranged because I knew he liked remembering where things were. His home did not feel larger because I'd entered it. It felt inhabited by two separate people who had learned where not to intrude.\n\n\"I should tell you something,\" I said.\n\nHe looked up.\n\n\"I'll be seeing Greg after tomorrow's archive shift. We planned dinner last week. I forgot to put it on the kitchen calendar.\"\n\n\"Do you need to leave earlier than you expected?\"\n\n\"Yes. About an hour.\"\n\n\"Then write it down.\"\n\nI did, and waited for the edge in his voice that never came.\n\n\"I'm not asking you to approve,\" I said. \"I just don't want you to plan an evening with me that I'm not available to keep.\"\n\n\"Good,\" he answered. \"I should find it profoundly irritating if you required my permission to honor another commitment.\"\n\n\"Would you?\"\n\n\"Yes. It would imply I hadn't been listening to you.\"\n\nI smiled.\n\nSeverus watched my face for a moment.\n\n\"Are you worried I mind?\"\n\n\"Sometimes. Not because you've asked me to choose. Because I know being excluded from a plan can hurt, and I don't want to use privacy as an excuse to hide something that affects us.\"\n\nHe set the book aside.\n\n\"I would rather be told what changes our plans than given a report on every moment you spend elsewhere.\"\n\n\"That sounds like our agreement.\"\n\n\"It is. We are apparently capable of remembering it.\"\n\nI laughed. Then I reached for his hand, leaving him room to refuse. He took mine.\n\n\"Would you like to spend the rest of this evening ignoring the archive?\" I asked.\n\n\"An ambitious proposal.\"\n\n\"Ten minutes at a time?\"\n\nHe kissed my knuckles.\n\n\"I think I can manage.\"\n\nAfter an hour, Severus shut his book.\n\n\"Are you comfortable?\" he asked.\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"Would you prefer the fire higher?\"\n\n\"I'm fine.\"\n\nHe studied me.\n\n\"That wasn't an invitation to endure the cold out of politeness.\"\n\nI laughed.\n\n\"All right. A little higher would be nice.\"\n\nHe adjusted the fire with a small spell, then returned to the chair.\n\nI watched him.\n\n\"What?\" he asked.\n\n\"Nothing.\"\n\n\"Jasper.\"\n\n\"I like seeing you relaxed.\"\n\nHe looked almost offended.\n\n\"I am not relaxed.\"\n\n\"Your shoulders have dropped.\"\n\n\"They are merely tired.\"\n\n\"Your book is on the wrong shelf.\"\n\nHis eyes widened.\n\nI laughed before he could find a defense.\n\nHe stood, crossed the room, and took the book from my hands.\n\n\"You are becoming intolerable.\"\n\n\"I've had a good teacher.\"\n\nHe put the book aside.\n\nThen he sat beside me.\n\nThe sofa was narrow. Our knees touched. I could feel the warmth of him through the fabric of his trousers, and the scent of old paper and tea clung to his sleeves.\n\n\"May I?\" he asked, lifting a hand toward my face.\n\nI nodded.\n\nHis fingers brushed a strand of hair from my cheek. The gesture was so careful that my chest tightened.\n\n\"You don't have to ask every time,\" I whispered.\n\n\"I know.\"\n\n\"Then why do you?\"\n\n\"Because I enjoy hearing you choose.\"\n\nI leaned toward him.\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\nHe kissed me slowly, then drew back just far enough to see my face. There was no urgency. No need to prove we'd survived another difficult conversation. We'd made time, kept it, and arrived at this moment on purpose.\n\nI kissed him again.\n\nWhen the rain grew heavier, he asked whether I wanted to stay.\n\n\"I do.\"\n\nWe left the books where they were, turned down the lamps, and closed the bedroom door. The rest of the evening remained ours.\n\nIn the morning, the kettle was empty and the bread was still terrible.\n\nSeverus made tea.\n\nI made porridge.\n\nHe glanced at the calendar pinned beside the kitchen door.\n\n\"Moonroot at ten,\" he said.\n\n\"I know.\"\n\n\"Neri wishes to review the revised catalog access forms.\"\n\n\"I know.\"\n\n\"Orin is due at eleven.\"\n\n\"Severus.\"\n\nHe looked at me.\n\n\"Yes?\"\n\n\"I can read.\"\n\nHis mouth twitched.\n\n\"An ability I have never questioned.\"\n\nI kissed his cheek and reached for the porridge.\n\nWe arrived at the archive on time. Neri had the new forms ready, with separate spaces for public engineering records and private correspondence. Orin arrived carrying a revised essay that no longer began by calling Severus a saint.\n\nThe work was difficult and ordinary.\n\nAt noon, I found the note with the criminal comma on the table.\n\nSeverus had corrected it again.\n\nThis time he'd added a small line beneath the margin.\n\nI LIKE YOUR SENTENCES EVEN WHEN THEY ARE WRONG.\n\nI looked toward his desk.\n\nHe refused to look up.\n\nI wrote underneath:\n\nAND I LIKE YOUR BOOKS EVEN WHEN YOU PUT THEM IN THE WRONG ORDER.\n\nHe made a disapproving sound.\n\nIt was a very good morning.","word_count":1432},"after-always-50":{"chapter_number":50,"title":"After the Door Opens","content":"The last chapter of the archive report began with a blank line.\n\nNeri had left it there deliberately, a space where the erased signature from the Closed Bells ledger might one day be identified through evidence or voluntary testimony. The committee had finally accepted that not knowing was different from refusing to care.\n\nSeverus stood beside the display case as the first visitors entered.\n\nHe had volunteered an hour of cataloging guidance each week, not because anyone had demanded it, but because he wanted the archive to become a place where students could learn how not to turn uncertainty into fiction.\n\nOrin was the first to arrive.\n\n\"I have a question,\" they said.\n\nSeverus sighed.\n\n\"An auspicious beginning.\"\n\nOrin smiled and opened their notebook.\n\nThey had revised the wartime biography once more, replacing grand claims with citations, conflicting testimony, and a section about people whose accounts remained incomplete. They didn't ask Severus to approve every sentence. They asked whether the evidence had been represented fairly.\n\nHe read the opening.\n\nThen he nodded.\n\n\"Better.\"\n\nOrin looked as though they might faint from happiness.\n\nI took the tea tray to the small table by the window, where Neri had laid out the public maintenance records. The door to the private correspondence room remained closed. The emergency structural records were available through the new sign-out system, and the rules were posted at a height visitors could actually read.\n\nIt wasn't glamorous.\n\nIt was working.\n\n\"Tea?\" I asked Severus.\n\nHe took a cup and sniffed it.\n\n\"What have you done?\"\n\n\"I made tea.\"\n\n\"That much is apparent.\"\n\n\"Is it terrible?\"\n\n\"It is an ambitious assault on the leaves.\"\n\nI laughed.\n\nHe drank it anyway.\n\nOutside, the first light of morning moved across the stone arches. People were gathering for the reopening of the safe canal path, though the lower service passage remained sealed pending more inspection. The square smelled of fresh bread and damp leaves.\n\nIroh passed the window carrying a small kettle. Greg was helping set up chairs. Danse spoke with Toph beside the bridge plan, both of them apparently capable of disagreeing without threatening each other's competence. Maui was carrying a crate of lantern hooks with an expression of remarkable restraint.\n\nSix different lives, overlapping without collapsing into one.\n\nSeverus watched them through the glass.\n\n\"You have a busy day,\" he said.\n\n\"I do.\"\n\n\"Are you coming back for dinner?\"\n\n\"Yes. If the inspection runs long, I'll send word before the time we agreed.\"\n\nHe nodded.\n\nThe simplicity of it still mattered.\n\nWe had talked about ethical non-monogamy and my preference for polyamory before the first fragile steps of romance. We'd revisited the agreements when schedules changed, when privacy felt like hiding, and when either of us feared being forgotten. There was no final answer that could make those conversations unnecessary forever.\n\nThere was only the decision to keep having them with care.\n\nSeverus set down the cup.\n\n\"Would you like to see the new catalog heading?\" he asked.\n\n\"Of course.\"\n\nHe showed me a card labeled PRIVATE MATERIAL — CONSENT REQUIRED.\n\nUnderneath, in smaller handwriting, he'd added: HISTORICAL INTEREST DOES NOT CANCEL OWNERSHIP.\n\nI looked at him.\n\n\"That's good.\"\n\n\"I am capable of useful work.\"\n\n\"I've noticed.\"\n\nHe leaned closer.\n\n\"Your punctuation, however—\"\n\n\"Severus.\"\n\n\"—remains a danger to public safety.\"\n\nI laughed.\n\nHe smiled.\n\nThe afternoon passed with visitors asking questions. Some were angry about the blank line. Some wanted a villain's name, a simple answer, or a story that made the valley's past easier to admire. Orin explained why the evidence did not yet support one.\n\nSeverus listened.\n\nHe didn't take over when the apprentice hesitated.\n\nThat might have been the most important lesson he'd learned to teach.\n\nNear the end of the visit, an older man arrived with a weather-stained ledger leaf wrapped in cloth.\n\n\"My mother kept this,\" he told Orin. \"It says a gate was repaired two days before the flood. Does that prove the keeper knew what would happen?\"\n\nOrin's hand hovered over the cloth.\n\nSeverus was listening from the other side of the room. I saw him take a breath and stop himself from answering first.\n\n\"We can look at the date,\" Orin said, \"but not at the paper without your permission. Is this an item you'd like to donate, or are you asking for help understanding it?\"\n\n\"Just help.\"\n\n\"Then you can keep it.\"\n\nThey placed a clean support on the table so the fragile page could rest without being pressed flat. The ink was faded, but the entry remained legible: one hinge replaced, one length of rope purchased, one signature abbreviated.\n\nOrin compared it with a copy of the public repair ledger.\n\n\"This shows maintenance happened,\" they said. \"It doesn't establish what someone knew about the later flood. We would need the weather reports, the instructions issued at the time, and enough context to interpret the abbreviation.\"\n\nThe man looked disappointed.\n\n\"I thought it would be the answer.\"\n\n\"So did I,\" Orin admitted. \"When you brought it in.\"\n\nSeverus stepped forward at last.\n\n\"That is why we are learning to stop before the conclusion,\" he said.\n\nOrin looked relieved but not rescued. They asked the visitor whether a non-identifying transcription could be added to the public evidence list. The man agreed to a copy, declined to donate the original, and asked for a receipt confirming the arrangement.\n\nWe gave him one.\n\nAfter he left, I found Severus standing before the now-empty support.\n\n\"That was difficult for you,\" I said.\n\n\"What was?\"\n\n\"Letting Orin work through the question.\"\n\nHe gave me a dry look.\n\n\"I found seven imprecisions and two potentially catastrophic assumptions.\"\n\n\"How many did you correct?\"\n\n\"One, when invited.\"\n\nI smiled.\n\nHe looked toward the corridor where the apprentice was filing the transcription.\n\n\"They will make mistakes,\" he said.\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"And they'll have to repair them.\"\n\n\"Also yes.\"\n\nHe studied me.\n\n\"You are distressingly content with imperfect people.\"\n\n\"Only when they let me be one too.\"\n\nFor a moment his expression softened in a way I might once have missed.\n\nHe reached for my hand. I let him take it.\n\nAt closing time, Neri locked the display cases and left the public light on beside the reading shelf. Anyone with a permitted request could return during the next posted hours. The archive would not be held open by one exhausted person's endless willingness to help.\n\nSeverus collected his coat.\n\n\"Ready?\" he asked.\n\nI nodded.\n\nWe walked through the Moonroot Door into Spinner's End. The kitchen was warm, and the table held two bowls, clean cups, and a loaf of bread that hadn't yet been subjected to either of our cooking.\n\nHe had remembered.\n\nI set down the keys and took off my coat.\n\n\"How did the hour go?\" I asked.\n\n\"Orin was tiresome.\"\n\n\"Did you enjoy it?\"\n\n\"Immensely.\"\n\nI smiled.\n\nHe reached for my hand.\n\n\"May I kiss you?\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\nThe kiss was tender and familiar, no longer a promise that he would become someone else, but a choice to meet me as he was. He held me close for a moment, then let me step back to stir the soup.\n\nWe ate while the rain began again outside.\n\nLater, he read by the fire and I wrote in my green notebook. I didn't write a declaration of forever. I wrote what had happened, what we had chosen, and the small ordinary things I wanted to remember.\n\nThe door to the archive would open again tomorrow.\n\nSo would the door to this house, whenever either of us chose to enter.\n\nI watched Severus turn a page and smile at something only he could see.\n\nThen I returned to my own book, grateful that neither of us needed to be a legend to be loved.","word_count":1292}},"love-and-duty":{"love-and-duty-49":{"chapter_number":49,"title":"Four People, Four Answers","content":"The rumor reached the house before breakfast.\n\nSomeone had told a visitor that our household required every member to approve every romantic decision Jasper made. Another person had added that the arrangement meant all four of us were always together. By the time the story reached Mara's bakery, a stranger was confidently explaining the rules of a relationship they had never asked about.\n\nToph heard the entire account while waiting for bread.\n\nShe came home furious.\n\n\"Apparently,\" she said, slamming the door, \"we're a committee.\"\n\nAang looked up from the kitchen table.\n\n\"What?\"\n\n\"Somebody thinks our love life has minutes and a voting chair.\"\n\nKatara set down her cup.\n\n\"Who said that?\"\n\n\"Does it matter? Half the square is repeating it.\"\n\nI felt my stomach tighten.\n\nI had told each person I cared about that I was ethically non-monogamous and preferred polyamory. We had discussed informed consent, individual relationships, privacy, time, and the right to change our minds. We had never agreed that one partner could vote on another person's autonomy.\n\nThe rumor turned a careful collection of choices into a spectacle.\n\n\"What do you want to do?\" I asked.\n\nToph folded her arms.\n\n\"Tell them where they can put their opinions.\"\n\n\"That's one option.\"\n\n\"It's a good option.\"\n\nKatara looked at me.\n\n\"Before we decide what to say publicly, we should check what each of us is comfortable sharing.\"\n\nAang nodded.\n\nToph sighed.\n\n\"Fine. A meeting about not having meetings.\"\n\nI almost laughed.\n\nWe didn't begin with a group statement. We began with individual conversations.\n\nAang found me in the garden first. He was sitting beneath the potato kite I'd hung by the window, looking uncharacteristically serious.\n\n\"I don't mind people knowing I love you,\" he said. \"I do mind when they assume I can speak for Katara or Toph.\"\n\n\"I know.\"\n\n\"Sometimes I worry that being the Avatar makes people treat my answer as the household's answer.\"\n\n\"Then say it's yours.\"\n\nHe smiled faintly.\n\n\"That sounds simple.\"\n\n\"It isn't always.\"\n\nHe took my hand.\n\n\"I want to be open about us. But I don't want our private disagreements turned into stories people use to decide whether polyamory can work.\"\n\n\"Neither do I.\"\n\nToph found me next.\n\nShe didn't sit. She paced the room once, then stopped.\n\n\"I don't care if people think I'm difficult,\" she said.\n\n\"I know.\"\n\n\"I care when they pretend I agreed to things nobody asked me about.\"\n\n\"Then what do you want them to know?\"\n\n\"That I choose my own relationships. That I like you. That I don't need permission from Aang to go on a date with you, and he doesn't need mine to ask for his time.\"\n\nI nodded.\n\n\"And what stays private?\"\n\nShe hesitated.\n\n\"The stuff that's ours.\"\n\nI smiled.\n\n\"That's fair.\"\n\nShe leaned close enough to kiss my cheek, then looked embarrassed by her own tenderness.\n\n\"Don't make a big thing of that.\"\n\n\"Wouldn't dream of it.\"\n\nKatara came to me after lunch.\n\nShe looked tired, not from the rumor alone but from days of coordinating water distribution after the bridge closure.\n\n\"I want us to be honest,\" she said. \"But I don't want honesty to become a public inventory of who kisses whom.\"\n\n\"Neither do I.\"\n\n\"I also want to know if any of us is struggling. Not because we need a vote, but because we're responsible for how we treat one another.\"\n\nI took her hand.\n\n\"You're allowed to ask for care without asking for control.\"\n\nShe smiled.\n\n\"Good. Because I'd like an evening with you soon, and I don't want to apologize for asking.\"\n\n\"Then let's plan one.\"\n\nWe did.\n\nWhen we met together in the evening, there was no single speech that solved every concern.\n\nAang wanted openness without being made the household spokesman. Toph wanted to refuse the rumor loudly. Katara wanted a clear statement that did not disclose private details. I wanted the right to speak about my own identity without feeling as though every romantic decision belonged to the crowd.\n\nBefore we wrote anything, Toph dumped a pile of papers onto the table.\n\n\"These,\" she announced, \"are the things people tried to put in our mailbox because they think we're holding auditions for a relationship.\"\n\nAang picked up the top sheet.\n\nIt was an invitation to a town discussion titled *The Four-Person Marriage Question*. Beneath it someone had penciled our names as guest speakers without having asked us.\n\nKatara closed her eyes.\n\n\"Absolutely not.\"\n\n\"Finally,\" Toph said, \"a unanimous vote.\"\n\n\"Not a vote,\" I reminded her.\n\n\"Fine. Four separate absolutely-nots.\"\n\nWe laughed, but the laughter didn't entirely remove the sting.\n\nThe organizer had also asked for a private explanation of our sleeping arrangements, framed as a way to \"educate curious neighbors.\" I turned the paper over. There was no need to keep looking at it.\n\nAang studied the invitation.\n\n\"They may genuinely be confused,\" he said.\n\n\"They can be confused without making us a display,\" Katara answered.\n\n\"I know. I'm just trying to think how to answer without making them feel ashamed of asking.\"\n\nToph planted her heel on the floor.\n\n\"They should feel a little ashamed of asking where everybody sleeps.\"\n\nI looked at Aang. He had an instinct to make peace with people even when the cost would fall on somebody else's privacy.\n\n\"Do you want to write to them yourself?\" I asked.\n\nHe considered.\n\n\"Yes. I can explain that public consent matters in any relationship, but our home isn't an example they can demand access to.\"\n\nKatara nodded.\n\n\"And if you want to give an educational talk someday, that is your choice. It doesn't make you the spokesman for us.\"\n\nHe smiled faintly.\n\n\"Thank you.\"\n\nToph picked up another sheet.\n\n\"Somebody's asking whether I mind you having dinner with Danse.\"\n\nI could hear the anger under her humor.\n\n\"What do you want to do with it?\" I asked.\n\n\"Throw it in a river.\"\n\n\"That seems wasteful. It's good scrap paper.\"\n\nShe grinned.\n\nWe used the backs of the invitations for the household shopping list. Aang wrote rice beside a particularly intrusive question. Katara added soap. Toph dictated *more patience for idiots*, which I refused to put on the public list.\n\nWhen we finally drafted the notice, each of us read it privately before anyone posted it. No one was forced to sign. Aang asked for a wording change so it could not be mistaken for permission to interview Katara or Toph without their consent. Katara asked us not to imply that privacy meant secrecy. Toph insisted on leaving out the number of people who shared the house.\n\nI looked at the final page.\n\nIt was less dramatic than the rumor.\n\nIt was also ours.\n\nWe agreed on a short public notice.\n\nJasper and their partners are adults who make individual, informed relationship choices. No one in the household owns another person's consent. We do not discuss private relationship details with strangers.\n\nToph read it aloud.\n\n\"Needs more swearing.\"\n\nKatara laughed.\n\nAang looked relieved.\n\nI posted the notice the next morning with no names attached beyond mine. The others chose how they wanted to respond if someone asked them directly.\n\nMost people ignored it.\n\nA few looked embarrassed.\n\nOne person asked whether we could explain the arrangement as an example for their own relationship.\n\nI said we could talk about honesty and consent generally, but not give them access to our private lives.\n\nThey accepted that.\n\nThat evening, the house was quiet.\n\nKatara had made tea. Aang was repairing a kite frame. Toph was sitting near the open window, listening to rain on the path.\n\nI sat at the table with them and felt the complicated warmth of belonging to people who had not become one person simply because we cared for one another.\n\nToph reached for my hand.\n\n\"Friday still ours?\"\n\n\"If you want.\"\n\n\"I do.\"\n\nAang smiled.\n\n\"I've got tomorrow morning if you're free.\"\n\n\"I am.\"\n\nKatara looked at me.\n\n\"And you and I can have the evening we planned next week.\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\nThere was no vote.\n\nThere was only each person asking, answering, and making space.\n\nLater, when the lamps were low, I sat beside Katara and kissed her slowly. She laughed against my mouth when Aang called from the kitchen to ask where the spare tea cups lived.\n\n\"Second shelf,\" she called.\n\nToph groaned.\n\n\"He lives here. How does he not know?\"\n\nI laughed, and Katara kissed me again.\n\nThe private part of the evening belonged to the people who had chosen it. We closed the door without making the rest of our lives disappear.\n\nThe next morning, Toph found the public notice still on the board.\n\nSomeone had added a question below it: BUT WHO IS IN CHARGE?\n\nShe took the pencil and wrote:\n\nEACH PERSON. TRY IT.\n\nThen she came home for breakfast, extraordinarily pleased with herself.","word_count":1472},"love-and-duty-50":{"chapter_number":50,"title":"A Home with Doors","content":"The bridge reopened without a ribbon.\n\nThat was Toph's condition.\n\n\"No speeches until somebody actually walks across it,\" she said.\n\nDanse looked at the inspection report.\n\n\"Agreed.\"\n\nAang smiled.\n\n\"I was going to suggest a small ceremony.\"\n\n\"Of course you were.\"\n\nKatara folded her arms.\n\n\"Could we focus on the bridge?\"\n\nThe repair crew had replaced the weakened support and tested the ramp after two storms. Edda had checked the grade, turning space, traction, and handrail height. Sela had reviewed the revised drainage route, confirming that water now moved away from her garden instead of collecting against the wall. The cart drivers had tested their loads.\n\nThe test that nearly delayed the reopening wasn't the one on the engineers' list.\n\nIt was a puddle.\n\nEdda reached the last turn of the approach path and stopped. The rain had ended hours earlier, but water still collected between the paving stones at the ramp's lower edge. Anyone walking in heavy boots could step over it. Edda couldn't avoid it without leaving the marked route.\n\nShe looked at Tamsin.\n\n\"How deep?\"\n\n\"Less than an inch.\"\n\n\"That's not what I asked. Will my front wheels get stuck if the silt builds up after three storms?\"\n\nTamsin knelt and checked the slope.\n\n\"You're right. The drainage lip could catch debris.\"\n\nDanse reached for his pencil.\n\n\"We can add a maintenance requirement.\"\n\n\"We should also move the lip,\" Edda said. \"A requirement doesn't stop a wheel getting caught before the inspection happens.\"\n\nToph pressed a hand against the edge of the stone.\n\n\"The support can handle another inch of channel on this side. The water is already trying to go that way.\"\n\nAang began to lift his arms, and Katara caught his sleeve.\n\n\"Please don't bend the puddle away and call the design fixed.\"\n\nHe lowered his hands.\n\n\"I wasn't going to.\"\n\nToph laughed.\n\n\"Airbender's honor?\"\n\n\"Fine. I was going to.\"\n\nWe delayed the crossing by two hours and called the crew back. Nobody was happy about the delay. A cart driver complained that he'd already arranged his deliveries. Mara had bread cooling at the bakery. The clinic needed supplies.\n\nKatara looked at the schedule.\n\n\"We can send the urgent items by the safe ridge. It'll cost us time, not the patients' medicine.\"\n\nI took the delivery sheet and called the depot runner to explain the changes.\n\nSela helped mark where the excess runoff would go. She was unwilling to accept another convenient fix that sent water toward her garden. Tamsin adjusted the surface channel, Danse checked the new fasteners, and Toph tested the stone support again after the mortar set.\n\nEdda made the next test crossing.\n\nAt the repaired edge she stopped deliberately, turned, and tried again from the opposite direction.\n\n\"Better,\" she said.\n\nOnly then did Tamsin add the test to the report.\n\nThe delay wasn't a failure of the celebration. It was the reason the celebration could mean something.\n\nI looked toward the bridge, where people were beginning to gather, and felt relieved that no one had offered to carry Edda past the problem and declare it solved.\n\nThere was no one heroic signature at the bottom.\n\nThere were many names.\n\nTamsin read the final report aloud because the people who had done the work deserved to hear what had passed and what still needed maintenance. The bridge was safe for the listed loads. Heavy carts would continue using the ridge until the next inspection. The old lower service passage remained sealed.\n\nThat was the agreement.\n\nEdda approached the ramp first.\n\nNo one carried her. No one turned the crossing into a demonstration of someone's generosity. She moved at her own pace, stopped to test the handrail, and continued across.\n\nAt the far side she turned.\n\n\"Works,\" she said.\n\nToph grinned.\n\n\"Now we can have the speech.\"\n\nWe laughed.\n\nThere was no speech.\n\nThere was tea and bread, the sound of carts moving along the safer path, and a handful of children racing one another until Katara reminded them not to run into the people still crossing.\n\nI stood beside the railing and watched the neighborhood come back to life.\n\nAang slipped his hand into mine.\n\n\"Happy?\" he asked.\n\n\"Relieved.\"\n\n\"That's a kind of happy.\"\n\n\"Today it is.\"\n\nHe leaned against me.\n\nThe bridge had been a long argument. It had cost money, slowed deliveries, and forced people to admit that the original plan had overlooked those who used it. It would need maintenance again. Nothing about reopening made the past disappear.\n\nBut people could cross.\n\nThat was enough.\n\nBy evening our house was full of guests.\n\nMara had brought bread. Iroh arrived with tea. Greg was helping Pip hang a small set of lanterns near the garden gate. Maui came carrying a crate of vegetables and an expression of triumph until Toph made him put it down and wash his hands.\n\nSeverus appeared briefly with a copy of the updated maintenance records for Aang, then looked as though he regretted allowing so many people to see him.\n\nDanse inspected the back step while pretending he was merely passing through.\n\nI watched the people I cared about fill the rooms.\n\nIt would have been easy to make the evening into an enormous declaration that we had finally built a perfect home.\n\nWe hadn't.\n\nWe had built a place where people were allowed to disagree, leave, visit, and come back.\n\nKatara found me by the doorway.\n\n\"You've been standing here for a while,\" she said.\n\n\"Taking it in.\"\n\n\"Anything wrong?\"\n\n\"No.\"\n\nShe smiled.\n\n\"Then come eat.\"\n\nWe sat together at the table, though not everyone could fit at once. People took turns. Toph complained that the chairs were too close to the wall. Greg moved one without being asked to do everything else. Iroh poured tea for those who wanted it, then sat down to drink his own.\n\nMaui told a story about a heroic fish, with only a modest amount of exaggeration.\n\nSeverus disputed one detail.\n\nThe argument was magnificent.\n\nI laughed until my face hurt.\n\nAfter the guests left, the house returned to its ordinary size.\n\nAang washed the dishes. Toph swept the floor in a way that made it clear she considered the task an insult to her talents. Katara opened the windows to let the warm air out.\n\nI stood by the bedroom door with my green notebook in my hands.\n\nKatara noticed.\n\n\"Writing?\"\n\n\"Maybe.\"\n\nShe came beside me.\n\n\"About what?\"\n\n\"Doors.\"\n\nShe looked confused.\n\nI smiled.\n\n\"When I first came here, I thought finding a home meant knowing where I had to stay. Now I think it means knowing I can leave and still be welcome.\"\n\nKatara took my hand.\n\n\"That's a good thing to know.\"\n\nAang joined us.\n\n\"Are you going somewhere?\"\n\n\"Not tonight.\"\n\nToph appeared in the doorway.\n\n\"Then stop making a dramatic speech about it and come sit down.\"\n\nI laughed.\n\nWe sat together in the living room, each with our own place and no need to make the arrangement identical every night.\n\nAang rested his head against Katara's shoulder. Toph leaned against my side. I felt the quiet warmth of their presence and the strange relief of not needing to turn it into a permanent promise of sameness.\n\n\"Can I ask something?\" I said.\n\nToph groaned.\n\n\"Here comes the meeting.\"\n\n\"No meeting.\"\n\n\"Good.\"\n\n\"Are we happy with the way we're doing this?\"\n\nThere was a pause.\n\nAang smiled.\n\n\"Today? Yes.\"\n\nKatara nodded.\n\n\"Yes. And if something changes, we talk.\"\n\nToph squeezed my hand.\n\n\"Obviously.\"\n\nI looked at each of them.\n\n\"Good.\"\n\nLater I took the small key to my room from the hook by the door. It opened a place that belonged to me, not as a secret from the others but as a right I didn't have to earn.\n\nI placed it beside the green notebook.\n\nThe house had doors to the garden, the street, the Threadway, and the rooms where we slept. None of them existed to trap us inside.\n\nIn the morning, I woke to the sound of Toph arguing with the kettle.\n\nAang was making breakfast. Katara was reading a letter from the South.\n\nThe bridge was open, the village was working, and the people I loved were already finding new things to disagree about.\n\nI smiled.\n\nHome, I decided, sounded exactly like that.","word_count":1383}},"what-the-armor-leaves-behind":{"what-the-armor-leaves-behind-49":{"chapter_number":49,"title":"The Good Parts of an Order","content":"Danse wrote DO NOT FORCE A CROSSING at the top of the emergency checklist.\n\nThen he stared at the sentence for a long time.\n\n\"Too strong?\" I asked.\n\n\"No.\"\n\nHe tapped the page.\n\n\"Not strong enough.\"\n\nWe were working in Brasswatch Yard, where the emergency planning group had gathered after the generator failure and the storm at Tideglass. The lower service passage remained sealed. The repaired upper gate had not yet passed its final test. People needed a reliable way to find shelter or return home when the weather made ordinary routes unsafe.\n\nThe old emergency manual was full of instructions that assumed someone with a uniform could decide what happened to everyone else.\n\nDanse had already removed the section about identifying untrustworthy visitors by their origins.\n\nNow he was looking at the parts that said travelers should be moved through the nearest available Threadway regardless of preference.\n\n\"That's not how our doors work,\" I said.\n\n\"I know.\"\n\nHe looked at the paper.\n\n\"But in an emergency, there may not be time for a long discussion.\"\n\nKatara, who was reviewing the medical section, put down her pen.\n\n\"Then the procedure should make the questions simple before the emergency happens.\"\n\nNeri nodded.\n\n\"People can choose a preferred shelter or ask for a private departure plan in advance. We don't need to record why they choose it.\"\n\nEdda raised a hand.\n\n\"And the route has to be one people can actually use. A safe arch is not a safe evacuation route if the path to it has three steps and no alternative.\"\n\nDanse began writing.\n\nWe built the checklist around decisions that could be made quickly without stealing anyone's agency. First, identify immediate danger. Second, announce the available routes and their known conditions. Third, ask which option the person wants. Fourth, provide assistance only with consent, except for the narrow circumstances in which someone cannot respond and there is an immediate threat to life.\n\nEven those exceptions required a written review afterward.\n\nIt was less tidy than a chain of command.\n\nIt was better.\n\nRenn, now working as a training assistant, tested the instructions by pretending to be a frightened newcomer with a suitcase.\n\nDanse approached.\n\n\"The lower path is closed. The ridge route is open, and the hall has shelter. Would you like help getting there?\"\n\nRenn shook their head.\n\n\"I want to go through the east Threadway.\"\n\nDanse glanced at the map.\n\n\"That route is safe at present. I can show you the way.\"\n\nRenn looked surprised.\n\n\"That's it?\"\n\n\"That's it.\"\n\n\"What if I won't tell you where I'm going?\"\n\n\"You don't have to.\"\n\nRenn smiled.\n\n\"Good.\"\n\nThen Edda tested a different scenario.\n\n\"What if I can't use the ridge because the gravel is loose?\"\n\nDanse looked at the route sheet.\n\n\"We have a level alternate path marked in blue.\"\n\n\"And if it's flooded?\"\n\n\"Then we tell you. We don't conceal the risk to make the plan look complete.\"\n\nShe nodded.\n\n\"Now we're getting somewhere.\"\n\nWe ran six scenarios before lunch. In one, a child was separated from their caregiver. In another, a visitor declined assistance despite a safe shelter being available. In a third, someone wanted to remain in a secure home rather than join the crowd at the hall.\n\nNot every scenario had a neat answer.\n\nThat was the point.\n\nThe seventh test was not in Danse's draft.\n\nAsh requested it after lunch. They stood at the workshop doorway without entering, their coat pulled close against the rain.\n\n\"I want to know what happens when someone says they don't trust the people offering help,\" they said.\n\nDanse looked up.\n\n\"That isn't a failure of the plan.\"\n\n\"Then test it.\"\n\nRenn took the role of an emergency volunteer. Ash stood at the threshold as themself, not a cardboard stranger invented for practice.\n\n\"The lower road is unsafe,\" Renn began. \"There is shelter in the hall and a safe route to the eastern Threadway. What would you prefer?\"\n\n\"Neither. I don't know you.\"\n\nRenn hesitated.\n\nDanse almost spoke. I saw his mouth open.\n\nThen he let Renn think.\n\n\"Would you like the information in writing?\" Renn asked. \"You can read it and decide. You do not have to come with me.\"\n\nAsh nodded.\n\n\"And if I ask whether the person at the next station knows my name?\"\n\n\"I haven't asked you for one.\"\n\n\"Good.\"\n\nThey tried the scenario again with a frightened child who couldn't find a caregiver, and again with a traveler whose route was open but whose luggage was too heavy to carry. In each case the volunteer had to distinguish immediate danger from a reason to control somebody else's movements.\n\nThe checklist grew more precise.\n\nIt also grew longer.\n\nDanse looked at the page.\n\n\"I wanted a procedure anyone could remember in a crisis.\"\n\nAsh crossed their arms.\n\n\"Then make the first part easy to remember: tell the truth, offer choices, don't threaten people.\"\n\nRenn wrote it in the margin.\n\nI watched Danse accept the correction. The old reflex—to give a firm order, make everyone move, tidy the uncertainty into compliance—still lived in him. But he had stopped mistaking that reflex for the only kind of courage.\n\nAfter the volunteers left, I stood beside him at the workbench.\n\n\"How are you?\" I asked.\n\n\"Embarrassed.\"\n\n\"By?\"\n\n\"How simple the question was. Why should someone trust me because I say I am trying to help?\"\n\n\"Not everybody has the same reasons to trust you.\"\n\nHe stared at the page.\n\n\"Then the plan should not require it.\"\n\nI took his hand briefly.\n\nHe squeezed back, then went to revise the first line.\n\nBy afternoon the checklist had grown shorter. We removed phrases that required volunteers to interpret someone's loyalty, intentions, or worthiness. We replaced them with clear conditions and choices.\n\nDanse looked exhausted.\n\n\"You're frowning,\" I said.\n\n\"I'm remembering how many times I used to give orders without asking what they cost.\"\n\nI sat beside him.\n\n\"You can keep the useful skills.\"\n\nHe looked at me.\n\n\"Can I?\"\n\n\"Organization. Clear signals. Knowing how to keep people safe. None of those belong to the Brotherhood forever.\"\n\nHe thought about that.\n\n\"Then perhaps the good parts of an order are the parts that help someone choose.\"\n\nI smiled.\n\n\"Write that down.\"\n\nHe did.\n\nWe posted the final draft for public feedback. It wasn't approved immediately. Tamsin wanted the structural instructions reviewed. Katara requested more detail on medical assistance. Edda asked for the map to be available in multiple formats. Neri wanted the privacy rules explained in plain language.\n\nDanse accepted every comment.\n\nA year ago, I thought he might have interpreted that as an attack.\n\nToday he thanked them.\n\nThat evening we walked through the yard while the lanterns came on. The generator hummed quietly behind the workshop, operating within its posted load limit.\n\n\"May I ask something?\" he said.\n\n\"Of course.\"\n\n\"Do you feel safe with me?\"\n\nI stopped.\n\nIt would have been easy to say yes immediately and use the word as a blanket over everything difficult.\n\nInstead I thought about the doors he'd learned to leave open, the mistakes he'd admitted, and the ways we had negotiated time and privacy without pretending love meant ownership.\n\n\"I feel safe asking you to change,\" I said. \"And I feel safe telling you when something isn't right.\"\n\nHe looked at me.\n\n\"That means more than I expected.\"\n\nI took his hand.\n\n\"Do you feel safe with me?\"\n\nHe considered.\n\n\"Sometimes I'm still frightened you'll find my history too heavy.\"\n\n\"I know.\"\n\n\"But I don't feel required to hide that fear from you.\"\n\nI leaned closer.\n\n\"Good.\"\n\nHe kissed me beneath the workshop sign, warm and gentle. The repaired light above the door made a small circle of gold around us.\n\nIn the morning, Renn arrived with another suggestion.\n\nThe checklist needed a section for people who chose not to disclose personal information to volunteers.\n\nDanse added it without complaint.\n\nThere was always another useful question.","word_count":1323},"what-the-armor-leaves-behind-50":{"chapter_number":50,"title":"A Name Above the Door","content":"The old sign came down in two pieces.\n\nOne half still said RESTRICTED ACCESS. The other had been painted over so many times that the original letters showed through in the rain.\n\nDanse stood beneath the empty brackets with a screwdriver in his hand.\n\n\"I should have changed it months ago,\" he said.\n\n\"You changed the door first,\" I answered.\n\nHe looked toward the entrance.\n\nThe workshop was open. Not unguarded, not careless, but designed for the people who used it. Edda could reach the bell. Ash could close the private workroom from inside. Renn and Aster had signed the repair schedule with their actual hours. The emergency exit opened without waiting for a former soldier to approve it.\n\nThe sign was the last thing that still spoke in the old voice.\n\nTamsin arrived carrying a fresh board.\n\n\"What are we calling it?\" she asked.\n\nDanse looked at me.\n\nI raised an eyebrow.\n\n\"It's your workshop.\"\n\nHe looked surprised.\n\nIt had taken him a long time to understand that choosing a name did not require someone else to grant him a title.\n\nHe looked at the board.\n\n\"Brasswatch Open Workshop,\" he said.\n\nTamsin smiled.\n\n\"Good. I can fit that.\"\n\nShe painted the letters in a deep blue that matched the safe-route signs. Beneath them she added smaller words, approved by the people who worked there:\n\nREPAIRS • TRAINING • SHELTER BY CHOICE.\n\nDanse read the line.\n\n\"Should it say no rank required?\"\n\nRenn, standing nearby, laughed.\n\n\"Only if you promise not to invent a new one.\"\n\nDanse smiled.\n\n\"No ranks.\"\n\nWe raised the sign together.\n\nIt wasn't a ceremony. Tamsin held the ladder, Renn guided the brackets, and I passed up the screws. Danse did the final tightening, then climbed down and stepped back.\n\nThe board was level.\n\nThe door was open.\n\nFor a moment none of us spoke.\n\nThen Ash came out of the private room and looked at the sign.\n\n\"Better,\" they said.\n\nDanse nodded.\n\n\"Thank you.\"\n\nAsh did not hug him, forgive him, or tell him he'd become a different person. They went back inside to finish their work.\n\nHe let them.\n\nThat mattered.\n\nThe opening day drew a few people who had avoided the workshop before. One brought a broken kettle. Another asked whether there was a safe place to repair a wheeled cart. A young traveler wanted to learn how to read a pressure gauge.\n\nRenn ran the beginner's lesson. Danse watched for a while, then stepped away when it became clear they didn't need him hovering.\n\nI caught his expression.\n\n\"Hard?\" I asked.\n\n\"Very.\"\n\n\"You're doing well.\"\n\n\"An alarming statement.\"\n\nI laughed.\n\nThe young traveler's first lesson did not go as planned.\n\nRenn had set out a pressure gauge, two small hoses and a tray for collecting water. The student, Ilya's cousin Niko, followed every instruction carefully until the second fitting refused to catch. Their hands began to shake.\n\n\"I'm sorry,\" Niko said.\n\n\"What for?\" Renn asked.\n\n\"I've wasted the lesson.\"\n\nDanse stood a few steps away, very still.\n\nI could see the sentence he'd been trained to say years ago: *You were given clear instructions.* He had no intention of using it, but the struggle to find different words showed in his face.\n\nRenn took the gauge from Niko.\n\n\"The fitting is worn,\" they said. \"I should have checked it before the lesson.\"\n\nNiko looked surprised.\n\n\"It isn't my fault?\"\n\n\"It might have been a mistake even if it were,\" Renn said. \"That would be part of learning.\"\n\nDanse stepped closer.\n\n\"Would you like to see how we test whether a fitting is safe to reuse?\"\n\nNiko nodded.\n\n\"All right. We will start there.\"\n\nHe didn't take the tools and do the repair himself. He showed the student where the thread had flattened, then handed the piece back and waited while Niko compared it with the new fitting.\n\nThe student found the difference.\n\n\"That's why it won't catch.\"\n\n\"Correct,\" Danse said.\n\nThe word held no threat.\n\nI looked toward the open exit, where the safe-route sign had been set at a height Edda could read. People were working at their own benches, answering questions, arguing about measurements, earning their wages. A workshop rather than a barracks.\n\nLater Niko asked whether a lesson could be scheduled for next week.\n\nRenn checked the calendar.\n\n\"We can. I'll have a better fitting ready.\"\n\nDanse smiled.\n\nI found him afterward washing the used tools.\n\n\"You looked frightened when Niko apologized,\" I said.\n\n\"I was remembering what happened to people who made mistakes under my command.\"\n\nHe shut off the water.\n\n\"I can't change those moments.\"\n\n\"No.\"\n\n\"I can make sure this room doesn't repeat them.\"\n\nI put the dry cloth beside him.\n\n\"That sounds like a good reason to keep the door open.\"\n\nHe looked at me with a small, tired tenderness.\n\n\"One of several,\" he said.\n\nAt noon, Toph arrived to inspect the new support beam. She tapped the stone, listened for the familiar movement, and declared the repair satisfactory under the conditions of the test.\n\nDanse checked the written measurement.\n\nIt matched.\n\n\"Almost competent,\" Toph said.\n\nHe smiled.\n\n\"High praise.\"\n\nShe grinned and left before he could thank her.\n\nThe afternoon moved on. A lantern flickered at the edge of the yard, and someone asked Pip to replace the wick. Mara delivered bread for the volunteers. The generator ran within its safe load.\n\nNothing dramatic happened.\n\nI thought Danse might be disappointed.\n\nInstead he looked almost peaceful.\n\nAfter closing, we sat on the back steps with two cups of tea.\n\nHe stared at the new sign.\n\n\"I spent years believing I knew who I was because I wore armor,\" he said.\n\n\"And now?\"\n\n\"Now I know what I want to do when I take it off.\"\n\nI looked at him.\n\n\"Repair things?\"\n\n\"Yes. Teach what I know. Learn what I don't. Make room for people who need shelter without demanding their names as payment.\"\n\nI felt warmth move through my chest.\n\n\"That sounds like a life.\"\n\nHe looked toward me.\n\n\"Would you like to be part of it?\"\n\n\"I already am.\"\n\nHe smiled.\n\n\"And you have other lives, other people you love. I know we agreed to that. I know you're ethically non-monogamous and prefer polyamory. I don't want to ask you to abandon any of it.\"\n\n\"Then what are you asking?\"\n\n\"For the chance to keep choosing this with you. The ordinary days. The difficult talks. The mistakes we correct.\"\n\nI took his hand.\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\nHe looked almost relieved enough to laugh.\n\n\"That's a lot of responsibility.\"\n\n\"Good thing you're capable of writing a checklist.\"\n\nHe laughed.\n\nI kissed him, slow and warm. His hand rested against the back of my neck, gentle and sure. We stayed close until the light above the workshop door came on and the yard grew quiet.\n\nLater, inside, the rest of the evening belonged to us. No rank, no audience, no need to make the tenderness visible to anyone else.\n\nIn the morning, Danse opened the workshop at the posted hour.\n\nRenn was already there with a student who wanted to learn how to replace a worn hinge. The kettle from yesterday had been repaired and was ready to collect. The emergency exit was clear.\n\nDanse put on his work gloves.\n\n\"Morning,\" he said.\n\n\"Morning,\" Renn answered.\n\nThey handed him the inspection sheet.\n\nHe read it, made one correction, and gave it back.\n\nThen he stepped aside so the student could begin.\n\nOutside, the sign caught the early light.\n\nBrasswatch Open Workshop.\n\nA name he'd chosen.\n\nA door anyone could ask to enter.\n\nA place he could leave at the end of the day and still choose to return.","word_count":1281}},"stars-between-the-strings":{"su-36":{"chapter_number":36,"title":"When We Choose Each Other","content":"The string lights were crooked.\n\nOne loop dipped low enough to brush Greg's hair. Another had been wound around the same post three times because someone had changed their mind about where the party ought to end. The band was late, the paper plates were trying to escape in the wind, and Greg was standing in the middle of the Beach City square with a microphone he had no intention of using.\n\n\"You're staring,\" he said.\n\n\"I'm assessing the danger.\"\n\n\"The lights?\"\n\n\"Your haircut.\"\n\nHe grinned and tucked a loose strand behind his ear.\n\nThe celebration had started as a small gathering after a community cleanup. People had carried away storm debris, repaired the picnic tables, and borrowed the church's folding chairs for the evening. Greg had volunteered music for one set; I had promised to help lay out the food. Neither of us had agreed to be the event's romantic headline.\n\nThen a local newspaper had asked for a photograph.\n\nThe reporter, a young woman named Ellis, approached with a camera and a folded page of interview notes. She looked nervous.\n\n\"Greg, can I get one shot of you and Jasper? People have been talking about you two.\"\n\nGreg's smile thinned, almost invisibly.\n\n\"Depends what the story's about,\" he said.\n\n\"The cleanup. And, well, the relationship. It's kind of sweet. Maybe a little unconventional.\"\n\nI set down a stack of cups.\n\n\"Unconventional how?\"\n\nEllis glanced at her notes.\n\n\"Someone said you travel a lot, Jasper. That you see other people.\"\n\nGreg looked at me, not to make me answer but to check whether I wanted to.\n\n\"I do,\" I said. \"I'm ethically non-monogamous and prefer polyamory. Everyone I'm involved with knows that, and each relationship is based on informed choice.\"\n\nEllis nodded quickly.\n\n\"So you aren't serious with Greg?\"\n\nMy stomach dropped.\n\n\"That's not what I said.\"\n\nShe blushed.\n\n\"Sorry. I didn't mean—\"\n\n\"I know. But I'd like you to ask what we actually mean instead of choosing a story where somebody must be temporary.\"\n\nGreg exhaled.\n\n\"I was about to say something stupid,\" he admitted.\n\n\"What kind?\"\n\n\"Something about being lucky to have whatever time you can spare for me.\"\n\nI looked at him.\n\n\"Greg.\"\n\n\"I know. It sounds kind of selfless until you realize it makes me look like a guy standing around hoping you'll pick him.\"\n\nEllis lowered her camera.\n\n\"I can leave the relationship out of the article.\"\n\n\"That would be my preference,\" I said. \"You can write about the cleanup. Ask the people who worked on the drainage ditch. Ask who hauled the chairs. Don't turn our private time into a test for strangers.\"\n\nEllis nodded.\n\nThen she asked, \"Can I photograph the repaired picnic table?\"\n\n\"Please,\" I said.\n\nShe went to find the volunteers.\n\nFor a moment neither Greg nor I spoke. The wind lifted the napkins, and we had to chase them down the path in a ridiculous little scramble.\n\nGreg caught the last one against his chest.\n\n\"Great start,\" he said.\n\n\"You're very dignified.\"\n\n\"I've been told.\"\n\nWe returned to the tables. He straightened the napkins while I tried to decide whether to bring up the line he'd almost said. I didn't want to leave it sitting between us like a broken instrument neither of us wished to touch.\n\n\"About being grateful for whatever time I can spare,\" I said.\n\nHe went still.\n\n\"I know.\"\n\n\"Do you actually feel that way?\"\n\n\"Sometimes.\" He looked at his hands. \"I don't want to make you smaller. I like that you have places to go and people to see. I also don't want to pretend I never miss you.\"\n\n\"Then tell me when you miss me. That's allowed.\"\n\n\"Is it?\"\n\n\"Yes. I'm your partner, not a holiday rental.\"\n\nHe laughed, startled.\n\n\"Okay. That's fair.\"\n\nI stepped closer.\n\n\"I choose you when I'm here. I also choose to be honest when I have plans elsewhere. Those aren't competing truths.\"\n\nHe set the stack of napkins down.\n\n\"And I can ask for time?\"\n\n\"You can. I can say yes or no. You get to say no to me too.\"\n\nHe looked at me with an expression both embarrassed and intensely fond.\n\n\"Pretty sure I'm going to need practice.\"\n\n\"Me too.\"\n\nSteven arrived carrying a cooler, rescued the conversation by tripping over a cable, and promptly asked why the band hadn't started yet.\n\n\"Artistic suspense,\" Greg said.\n\n\"You're not even playing yet.\"\n\n\"Exactly.\"\n\nSteven shook his head and helped set the cooler down. He had brought enough cold drinks for the volunteers. Greg asked whether he wanted to stay for the music; Steven said yes, then went to help move chairs.\n\nNo one turned him into a witness to our romantic discussion.\n\nA little later Ellis returned with the article draft. She had removed the guesses about our relationship and added the names of the volunteers who had repaired the square. She asked us to check our direct quotations before printing.\n\n\"Better,\" I said.\n\nGreg smiled.\n\n\"Look, someone actually wrote about the storm drain. This town is growing up.\"\n\nThe band finally arrived. Their drummer apologized for being late; a delivery truck had blocked the road. Greg helped carry the cases but did not volunteer to manage the whole set. We hung the last string of lights high enough for people to walk beneath without ducking.\n\nWhen the first song began, Greg reached for my hand.\n\n\"Want to dance?\"\n\n\"I thought you'd never ask.\"\n\n\"I'm trying this new thing called asking.\"\n\n\"Very advanced.\"\n\nHe laughed and drew me into the uneven little space between the tables. We moved badly at first, bumping shoulders and laughing when the rhythm changed. He didn't pull me closer until I did, and when his thumb brushed my knuckles I felt the warmth of the choice we'd made in front of nobody who needed to know.\n\nAcross the square, the lights shone on the volunteers, the repaired tables, and the people eating too much cake. There was no grand declaration. We didn't ask the band to play a song about us.\n\nWhen Greg kissed me, it was brief and sweet. I leaned against him, and he rested his cheek against my hair.\n\n\"Can we keep doing this?\" he asked.\n\n\"The terrible dancing?\"\n\n\"That, yes. But I meant being honest before I start making jokes instead.\"\n\nI smiled.\n\n\"Let's practice.\"\n\nWe left the square after the cleanup shift we'd agreed to, not after every person had gone home. Steven promised to help the last volunteers collect the chairs. Ellis gave us a copy of the revised article, now mostly about the town's flood drains and the people who fixed them.\n\nGreg tucked it under his arm.\n\nAt the van, he stopped and looked back at the crooked lights.\n\n\"Should we fix that loop?\"\n\n\"Tomorrow.\"\n\n\"Really?\"\n\n\"Really.\"\n\nHe kissed my cheek.\n\nThe next morning we returned with a ladder and a proper set of clips. The lights hung straight for exactly one afternoon before the sea breeze twisted one of them sideways again.\n\nGreg looked up.\n\nI looked at him.\n\nWe laughed.\n\nThe crooked loop stayed until the next scheduled repair, and our relationship did not depend on the entire town approving the shape of it.","word_count":1202},"su-37":{"chapter_number":37,"title":"The Quiet Encore","content":"The last audience member had gone home, but Greg was still working.\n\nHe was coiling a cable when I found him. The Starwake stage stood empty, its lanterns dark except for the safety light near the steps. Someone had left a paper cup under the bench. The wind made it roll in small circles.\n\n\"Are we done?\" I asked.\n\n\"Almost.\"\n\n\"That's what you said twenty minutes ago.\"\n\n\"Time is a mysterious concept to musicians.\"\n\nHe grinned without looking up.\n\nGreg had agreed to play one set at the Listening Fair. He played beautifully. Then he'd helped carry chairs, checked the microphone, found missing keys and offered to stay until every piece of equipment was packed.\n\nThe actual cleanup volunteers were standing nearby, waiting for him to hand over the last two cables.\n\nI touched his elbow.\n\n\"Greg, they're here.\"\n\nHe looked at the workers.\n\n\"Oh. Right.\"\n\nNola, the shift leader, held out her hand.\n\n\"We've got the equipment from here. That was the agreement.\"\n\n\"Sure, but I can help with—\"\n\n\"Your shift ended.\"\n\nHe stopped and gave her the cable. She smiled.\n\n\"Thanks for the music. Go rest.\"\n\nWe walked to the safe bench along the pier. The western boards had passed inspection with posted limits. The strange low note beneath the supports hadn't sounded that evening; Fen's tide chart showed an ordinary tide. Beyond the lanterns, the sea moved in dark folds.\n\nGreg sat and stared toward the empty stage.\n\n\"You were quiet during the last song,\" he said.\n\n\"I liked it.\"\n\n\"Was it too slow?\"\n\n\"No.\"\n\n\"Too sad?\"\n\n\"Greg.\"\n\nHe looked at me.\n\n\"Are you asking whether I liked the music, or whether I liked you?\"\n\nHe laughed weakly.\n\n\"Probably both.\"\n\nI sat beside him.\n\n\"I liked the music. I love you. I'm allowed to be quiet while I listen.\"\n\nHe smiled, then looked embarrassed.\n\n\"Everybody goes home when the music stops,\" he said. \"I used to think if I kept the lights on long enough, no one would forget me.\"\n\n\"Is that why you keep doing everyone else's cleanup shifts?\"\n\n\"Maybe.\"\n\n\"Does it help?\"\n\nHe looked at his hands.\n\n\"No. Mostly it makes me tired and a little resentful nobody knows I'm tired.\"\n\n\"Have you told anyone?\"\n\n\"I'm telling you.\"\n\nI reached toward him, then paused.\n\n\"Want me close?\"\n\n\"Yeah.\"\n\nI moved closer. He put an arm around my shoulders, and we shared the blanket he'd brought for me.\n\n\"Did you bring one for yourself?\" I asked.\n\n\"No.\"\n\n\"Then this one is ours for a while.\"\n\nHe tucked it around both of us.\n\nGreg was generous with things he could offer without being asked. It took much more courage for him to admit he needed something. I recognized the habit. I had spent too many years hoping people would notice what I couldn't bring myself to say.\n\n\"Can I ask you something difficult?\" he said.\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"Do you ever worry I'm only happy when you need me?\"\n\n\"Sometimes I worry you use being useful to avoid saying what you want.\"\n\n\"That's probably fair.\"\n\n\"What do you want tonight?\"\n\nHe opened his mouth, then shut it.\n\n\"Not another concert.\"\n\n\"Good.\"\n\n\"Not a big talk about forever.\"\n\n\"Also good.\"\n\nHe looked almost shy.\n\n\"I want to sit here until the maintenance lights go out. Then maybe get something warm to eat. Have one night where neither of us needs to be interesting.\"\n\nMy chest tightened.\n\n\"I want that too.\"\n\n\"Wow. A perfectly matched set of glamorous wishes.\"\n\nWe laughed.\n\nNola finished locking the equipment shed and called a final goodnight. Greg thanked her without offering another shift. She had her own work and her own evening; we didn't have to become part of it.\n\nWe sat until the sea wind made my fingers cold.\n\n\"There's something else,\" Greg said.\n\nI looked at him.\n\n\"I know you've got other places to go. Other people you love. We talked about ethical non-monogamy and your preference for polyamory when this first became serious. I meant it when I said I wanted to make room.\"\n\n\"I know.\"\n\n\"Sometimes when you leave for another date, I feel lonely. Then I tell myself I shouldn't, because I agreed.\"\n\n\"Loneliness doesn't mean you've broken our agreement,\" I said. \"Neither of us promised never to feel afraid.\"\n\nHe let out a breath.\n\n\"Can I ask for next Sunday?\"\n\n\"Yes. We can plan that. I have Moonroot work in the morning, but the afternoon is free. If it runs late, I'll tell you.\"\n\nHe nodded.\n\n\"I'd like that.\"\n\nI rested my head against him. I could love someone without asking them to become the only person I loved. I could also take their loneliness seriously, without treating it as a demand to choose.\n\nOn the walk to the Starwake Door, Greg stopped to point out a tiny fish under the boardwalk, an upside-down lantern reflected in a puddle, and a scrap of paper caught on a nail.\n\n\"You're still working,\" I accused when he bent to pick up the paper.\n\n\"That was one piece of litter.\"\n\n\"An heroic intervention.\"\n\n\"Saved the entire pier.\"\n\nI laughed.\n\nAt the late stall, Mara served soup so hot it made Greg burn his tongue. He insisted the temperature proved its artistic intensity. She told him to drink water and stop giving speeches in her doorway.\n\nHe obeyed.\n\nWe split the last roll. He asked if I wanted to go home with him. I said yes because I wanted to, not as a reward for his music.\n\nAt home, we left his guitar in its case. The evening did not need a soundtrack. He kissed me slowly, one hand warm against mine. I caught his sleeve and kissed him again. Later we closed the door and left the rest of the night private.\n\nIn the morning, I found him making breakfast.\n\nNo guitar. No microphone.\n\nJust Greg trying not to burn toast.\n\n\"You look happy,\" I said.\n\n\"I am.\"\n\n\"Without an audience?\"\n\n\"Especially without one.\"\n\nHe handed me a piece of toast. It was almost perfect.\n\nI ate it anyway.\n\nLater, Nola sent a note thanking the volunteers for honoring their scheduled shifts. Greg pinned it beside the old list of shows he thought he had to accept.\n\nThe old list stayed.\n\nThe new note went above it.","word_count":1045},"su-38":{"chapter_number":38,"title":"A Future with Windows","content":"The new windows changed the room.\n\nNot magically. They let morning sunlight reach the far wall, where paint had faded behind an old poster. Dust floated through the bright strip of air, and a plant Greg insisted was \"pretty much immortal\" looked alarmingly alive for the first time in weeks.\n\n\"See?\" he said. \"A whole different future.\"\n\n\"The future needs watering.\"\n\n\"That too.\"\n\nWe had repaired the small room where Greg practiced near Starwake Pier. It wasn't a grand house or an exclusive promise. It was a place he could use freely and I could visit when I chose, with windows that no longer rattled in every storm.\n\nThe repairs had taken twice as long as expected. Our first measurements were wrong. The replacement frame arrived with a cracked pane. Tamsin supervised the final fitting, and Greg paid the invoice instead of offering to play three free concerts in exchange for someone else's labor.\n\nWe stood inside with a pencil, a notebook, and a half-eaten bag of crackers.\n\n\"What goes where?\" Greg asked.\n\n\"Music stand.\"\n\n\"Obviously.\"\n\n\"Chair near the window.\"\n\n\"Good.\"\n\n\"One shelf that isn't filled entirely with cables.\"\n\nHe looked wounded.\n\n\"You're asking me to give up my heritage.\"\n\n\"Your heritage includes seventeen identical adapters.\"\n\n\"Some of them are slightly different.\"\n\nI laughed and took the pencil.\n\nGreg drew a floor plan on the back of a grocery list. The room looked enormous in his sketch.\n\n\"That couch won't fit through the door.\"\n\n\"Sure it will.\"\n\n\"By bending space?\"\n\nHe checked the doorway, then crossed out the couch.\n\n\"Fine. A heroic folding chair.\"\n\nWe measured the walls properly. I wrote down the numbers while Greg double-checked them. We both remembered the first window frame.\n\nThen he found a picture of a wooden window seat and held it up like a proposal.\n\n\"Would you want one of these?\"\n\n\"Maybe. But how much does it cost? Would it block the door? Could someone still reach the window latch?\"\n\nHe studied the plan.\n\n\"You're right.\"\n\n\"And I don't want the room to become proof that I'm supposed to stay here forever.\"\n\nHis hand stopped.\n\n\"I didn't mean it that way.\"\n\n\"I know. That's why I'm telling you before we build something we can't undo.\"\n\nHe sat on the ledge, careful not to lean against the fresh frame.\n\n\"Sometimes I imagine your books and my guitar and enough space for Steven to visit,\" he said. \"Then I remember you have other people you love, other places to go, and I start worrying I'm planning a house nobody has agreed to live in.\"\n\nI sat beside him.\n\n\"You can imagine a future with me.\"\n\n\"Even if it isn't your only future?\"\n\n\"Yes. I'm ethically non-monogamous and I prefer polyamory. That doesn't mean I can't want a room here too. It means we need to talk about what the room represents.\"\n\nHe looked out the window.\n\n\"So what does it represent?\"\n\n\"A place you can call yours. A place where I can be welcome without being obligated to stay. A place where the people you love can visit by invitation, not assumption.\"\n\nHe smiled.\n\n\"I like that.\"\n\n\"And if I want a door I can close?\"\n\n\"Then you close it. So do I.\"\n\n\"Radical home design.\"\n\nWe laughed.\n\nAt the hardware shop, the clerk showed us a folding chair that fit, a low shelf, and wall hooks rated for the guitar case. Greg wanted a bright purple curtain.\n\n\"Absolutely not.\"\n\n\"You're stifling my artistic expression.\"\n\n\"Your artistic expression can have a sample swatch before it becomes our problem.\"\n\nWe took the swatches.\n\nBack at the room, Steven arrived with a potted plant.\n\n\"Thought the windows could use something green,\" he said.\n\nGreg stared.\n\n\"Is that a cactus?\"\n\n\"It's easy to care for.\"\n\n\"The last one died.\"\n\nSteven looked at me.\n\n\"He overwatered it.\"\n\n\"That tracks.\"\n\nGreg protested while we set the pot where it would get light without blocking the latch. Steven asked whether he could come by to record a song with his dad. Greg said yes, then checked the schedule instead of promising every day of the week.\n\nI watched them make the plan.\n\nThis was what I wanted: a room that could hold different kinds of love without demanding they all became the same thing.\n\nAfter Steven left, we reviewed the window seat. The budget was tight after the repairs, and the folding chair would do for now. We drew a dotted outline on the plan, with a note to reconsider it after the next maintenance fund review.\n\nGreg leaned on the doorframe.\n\n\"Do you think we're being too practical?\"\n\n\"What would too practical look like?\"\n\n\"Maybe a rule about how many times I can tell you I love you before the room gets boring.\"\n\nI looked at him.\n\n\"That's not a practical problem.\"\n\n\"Just a thought.\"\n\nI went to him and took his hand.\n\n\"You can tell me. I'm not going to measure it against the things I feel for other people.\"\n\nHe smiled.\n\n\"Good.\"\n\nHe kissed my forehead. We stayed there for a moment while the sea wind moved through the open window.\n\nThen the curtain sample blew off the sill.\n\nGreg lunged for it, tripped over the folding chair, and landed safely against the wall with an expression of wounded dignity.\n\n\"Majestic,\" I said.\n\n\"Exactly as planned.\"\n\nWe laughed until we had to sit down.\n\nBefore dusk we checked the exit was clear, the window latch worked, and the plant wasn't where the next storm would throw it onto the floor. Greg surveyed the room.\n\n\"Not much furniture.\"\n\n\"Enough.\"\n\n\"Plenty of future, though.\"\n\n\"You're starting to sound like a greeting card.\"\n\nHe laughed.\n\nI sat in the heroic folding chair. Greg perched on the shelf until I reminded him it wasn't a bench. He settled on the floor beside me.\n\n\"Comfortable?\" he asked.\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"Can I kiss you?\"\n\n\"Please.\"\n\nHe kissed me warmly, one hand resting against mine.\n\n\"Do you think this counts as building something together?\" he asked.\n\n\"Even without the window seat?\"\n\n\"Especially without it.\"\n\nWe turned down the lights and left the room tidy enough to use tomorrow. The rest of the evening stayed private, full of awkward kisses, laughter, and the freedom to fall asleep without performing a perfect future.\n\nThe next morning, the plant had survived.\n\nThe curtains remained undecided.\n\nThe future was not finished.\n\nBut the window opened smoothly, the door could close, and the people who loved this room were free to choose when to return.","word_count":1084},"su-39":{"chapter_number":39,"title":"The Song We Keep","content":"Greg had written the last line of the song three times.\n\nOn the first scrap of paper, it promised a forever neither of us had asked for.\n\nOn the second, it sounded like somebody leaving before they could be left.\n\nOn the third, the line stopped halfway through.\n\nHe put all three beside his guitar and stared at them.\n\n\"I think I've invented a new kind of writer's block,\" he announced.\n\n\"What's it called?\"\n\n\"Having feelings and being annoying about them.\"\n\nI laughed, then saw he wasn't quite joking.\n\nWe were in the garage, surrounded by cables, a small recording device, and a box of things Greg had been meaning to sort for months. The air smelled of warm dust and sea salt. One wall held posters from shows I'd never attended; another held a shelf with spare strings and the battered notebook where he kept chord progressions.\n\nThe song had begun beside Starwake Pier, where water made a low note beneath the wooden boards. Greg had copied part of that rhythm into the melody, leaving a long pause after the fifth note. He liked the pause when he played alone. Every time he tried to finish the lyrics, he filled it with a promise too large to trust.\n\n\"Play the beginning again?\" I asked.\n\nHe picked up the guitar.\n\nThe opening was gentle and slightly crooked. I knew the melody well enough to hum along, though I still missed the turn in the third phrase.\n\nGreg stopped.\n\n\"That's the wrong harmony.\"\n\n\"Sorry.\"\n\n\"No, wait. Do it again.\"\n\nI hummed.\n\nHe listened, then picked out a new chord beneath it.\n\n\"Oh,\" he said.\n\n\"Good oh?\"\n\n\"Interesting oh. That's the kind that gets you in trouble.\"\n\nHe played it again. The new chord made the next note sound uncertain, almost as though the tune had reached a doorway and didn't know whether to step through.\n\n\"Keep that,\" I said.\n\nGreg set down the guitar.\n\n\"You're not just saying that to be nice?\"\n\n\"No. I actually like the way it doesn't resolve.\"\n\nHe stared at the unfinished line.\n\n\"That's the problem.\"\n\nI waited.\n\n\"If I finish it,\" he said, \"people might think I'm promising something about us. Like the song's a contract.\"\n\n\"Do you think that?\"\n\n\"Sometimes I worry I'll start believing it.\"\n\nHe looked embarrassed, and I knew how much it had cost him to admit it. Greg was generous with laughter. Fear was harder. He tended to pack it inside a joke and hope the music would do the rest.\n\nI sat on the overturned crate beside him.\n\n\"We can write a song without making it a binding agreement.\"\n\n\"Sure. But then I get scared that if it's not a big promise, it doesn't mean enough.\"\n\nI thought about the green notebook in my bag, the one with my living agreements and the things I was learning to ask for.\n\n\"Can I tell you something without turning it into a verse?\"\n\nHe smiled.\n\n\"Please.\"\n\n\"I love you. I am ethically non-monogamous and prefer polyamory, and we've already talked about that honestly. I don't want the song to erase those choices. I don't want it to make you a consolation prize, either.\"\n\nGreg looked down at his hands.\n\n\"That's the part I'm bad at.\"\n\n\"Which part?\"\n\n\"Believing I matter without being the only one.\"\n\nI felt my chest tighten.\n\n\"I can't prove you matter by making somebody else disappear.\"\n\n\"I know.\"\n\n\"But I can tell you why you're here with me. Because I like your terrible jokes. Because you listen when I say a plan isn't working. Because you remember what I drink when I get distracted. Because you can be scared and still choose to tell me.\"\n\nHe breathed out slowly.\n\n\"That's a pretty good list.\"\n\n\"There's also the van.\"\n\n\"Ah. The real reason.\"\n\n\"The seats are surprisingly comfortable.\"\n\nHe laughed, and it eased something in the room.\n\nHe reached for my hand.\n\n\"Can I ask you for a date next week?\"\n\n\"Yes. I have an afternoon free after Moonroot.\"\n\n\"Just us?\"\n\n\"Just us. If the archive runs late, I'll send word instead of leaving you guessing.\"\n\n\"Deal.\"\n\nWe sat for a while, listening to the little recording device hum.\n\nThen I picked up the third page.\n\n\"What if the last line isn't about forever? What if it's about something we keep doing, one time at a time?\"\n\nGreg looked at me.\n\n\"Like what?\"\n\n\"Listening.\"\n\nHe considered.\n\n\"Too much like a public service announcement.\"\n\n\"Coming back?\"\n\n\"Too much like a ferry schedule.\"\n\nI grinned.\n\n\"You're impossible.\"\n\n\"Artistically sensitive.\"\n\n\"Same thing.\"\n\nHe played the melody again. This time he left the space open, and I hummed my wrong harmony over the next chord. It didn't resolve neatly. It sounded like two people who had heard each other and still had different ways of reaching the same place.\n\nGreg's eyes lit up.\n\n\"That's it.\"\n\n\"What?\"\n\n\"We don't need another line.\"\n\n\"Seriously?\"\n\n\"The space is the line.\"\n\nI stared.\n\n\"Greg.\"\n\n\"What?\"\n\n\"That's either brilliant or you've avoided writing words so successfully you've declared silence a lyric.\"\n\nHe grinned.\n\n\"Both can be true.\"\n\nWe tested the song on the little recording device. The first take had a creaking chair in the middle of the pause. The second caught the sound of a truck backing up outside. On the third, Greg missed a chord because I'd made him laugh.\n\nWe kept the third.\n\nIt sounded like us.\n\nLater we took the guitar down to the pier. The boards were dry, the safety notices were posted, and the wind carried the smell of the ocean through the railings. Greg checked the stage schedule with the volunteer steward before setting up. We weren't there to take over a public performance space; we wanted five minutes to hear how the new chord sounded outdoors.\n\nA young busker was already playing near the bench.\n\n\"Mind if we wait?\" Greg asked.\n\nThe musician shook their head.\n\n\"Not at all. I've got ten minutes left.\"\n\nWe listened until their set finished, then helped move the stand to storage. Greg played our song for the open air.\n\nThe last phrase hung between the sea and the wooden railing.\n\nNo triumphant resolution.\n\nNo tragic ending.\n\nA breath.\n\nI leaned against the post.\n\n\"What do you think?\" I asked.\n\nGreg looked at the page, then at me.\n\n\"I think I'm done trying to make this song answer questions it wasn't asked.\"\n\nI smiled.\n\n\"That's a good place to stop.\"\n\nHe set the guitar in its case.\n\n\"May I kiss you?\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\nHe kissed me warmly, one hand against my cheek, and pulled back with a laugh when a gust of wind blew his hair into my face.\n\n\"Very romantic.\"\n\n\"I was aiming for majestic.\"\n\n\"You hit ridiculous.\"\n\n\"Close enough.\"\n\nAt home, we put the third page in the song notebook. We kept the earlier drafts, too, because they were part of how we'd reached this version. Greg wrote a small note beside the empty final line:\n\n*Leave room for the next day.*\n\nThat evening we ate dinner from paper cartons and watched the light move across the garage floor. The rest of our time stayed private; no lyric, no performance, no audience.\n\nIn the morning, Greg played the unfinished song once more.\n\nHe stopped at the open space.\n\nThis time he smiled before the next chord.\n\nIt was finished enough to keep living.","word_count":1229}}};
